Recommended default: Cube Core + Metabase OSS + small orchestration service. ��� Cube Core gives you the API-first, headless semantic layer with REST, GraphQL, and SQL interfaces, while Metabase OSS gives you the fastest path to usable dashboards, ad hoc exploration, saved questions, filters, and CSV export. ���This split aligns well with your requirements because Cube handles governed metrics, joins, and reusable business definitions, while Metabase handles the “manual BI is primary” requirement with a mature UI and open-source self-hosting story. ��� The custom code you write can stay focused on schema introspection, LLM prompting, report suggestion, and translating natural-language edit intents into Cube model changes or Metabase question/dashboard mutations. ��Why Cube fitsCube Core is explicitly open source, self-managed, works with Postgres, and is headless by design with REST, GraphQL, and SQL APIs. ��� Its semantic layer model is the cleanest foundation for AI-assisted report generation because the LLM can operate on governed measures, dimensions, and joins instead of raw table guessing. ��Cube also gives you a Postgres-compatible SQL interface, which is useful because downstream BI tools can connect to Cube as if it were a database, letting you interpose the semantic layer without forcing every consumer to understand your raw schema. �� Its built-in Playground exists mainly for development and query prototyping, and query tabs can retain prior queries in browser local storage, but that is not a production-grade end-user BI UI. �Why Metabase fitsMetabase OSS covers the manual BI surface area well: saved questions, dashboards, filters, visualization, and API-accessible dataset export in CSV, JSON, or XLSX formats. �� It also has audit and usage data concepts that include query/view tracking and revision history for saved queries, which maps reasonably well to your state/history requirement. ���Its weakness for your use case is that the best modern embedding SDK is on paid tiers, and open-source embedding is more iframe-centric and less composable for deeply integrated product experiences. ��� If your “API-first & headless” requirement is strict and you expect your own application to create and manage BI assets heavily through APIs, Metabase OSS is good but not perfect. ���Where Superset may be betterApache Superset is a stronger choice if you care more about programmatic dashboard lifecycle management than ease of use for non-technical users. �� It exposes REST APIs for dashboard create, update, export, and import, and chart data can be downloaded as CSV through API endpoints. ��The tradeoff is product complexity: Superset is usually more “data platform” and less “instant self-service BI” than Metabase. �� So if your tenant user is business-facing and needs quick manual report creation, Metabase often wins; if your product is engineering-led and wants dashboards as managed artifacts, Superset can be the better shell around Cube. ��DynamicJasper’s roleDynamicJasper is open source and can generate JasperReports layouts programmatically, with export support including CSV, XLS, HTML, PDF, and more. � That makes it useful for pixel-controlled operational reports, scheduled document-style outputs, or Java-native report generation where you need code-defined templates. �But it is not a substitute for a modern semantic layer plus exploratory BI interface. � I would not use DynamicJasper as the center of this system unless your real requirement is “server-side printable reports with some charting,” not “self-hosted API-first BI with AI-assisted editing.” �Architecture to useUse this layering:A cohesive flow would look like this: your service connects to the tenant Postgres, introspects schema, generates or refines Cube models, exposes Cube as the governed query plane, and either points Metabase/Superset at Cube or uses their APIs to create questions/charts/dashboards automatically. ��� The LLM never becomes the query engine; it only suggests metrics, drafts report configs, and edits BI assets using your orchestration layer. ��How AI should workThe safest AI pattern is assistive configuration, not direct SQL generation against the tenant database. �� Give the model access to the Cube semantic definitions, allowed dimensions/measures, join graph, sample metadata, and BI asset schemas, then have it produce structured intents like “create dashboard,” “add monthly revenue by region,” or “replace raw table with governed measure.” ��Your orchestration service should validate every AI output against allowed semantic objects, then call Cube and the BI tool APIs to materialize the change. ��� This approach preserves manual BI as first-class, while using AI as an editor that manipulates the same underlying objects a human would. ��My recommendation by scenarioIf you want the least custom code, choose Cube Core + Metabase OSS. �� This is the most practical choice for delivering governed metrics, decent dashboards, CSV export, and basic history with only a thin AI orchestration layer. ��If you want the most API-manageable dashboard platform, choose Cube Core + Superset. �� You will likely spend more effort on UX and operational polish, but you gain a more automation-friendly BI surface. �If you also need formal, printable, highly controlled reports, add DynamicJasper as a sidecar reporting service, not as the BI core. � Think of it as the “document/report renderer” for invoices, board packs, or exports that need layout guarantees. �Final verdictFor your exact brief, I would architect it as Cube Core for the canonical semantic/API layer, Metabase OSS for out-of-the-box BI, and a custom AI orchestration service that reads Cube metadata and writes Metabase assets. ��� That is the cleanest way to hit your 90/10 goal without taking on the burden of building a BI engine yourself. ��If you want, I can next turn this into a concrete reference architecture with deployment topology, component boundaries, API contracts, and a build-vs-buy scorecard for Cube vs Metabase vs Superset vs Lightdash
